WEBVTT
Kind: captions

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.473 position:63%
[Translated by AI. Submit corrections at criblate.com]
This animation is previewing an algorithm that can compress large pieces of text down to

00:00:04.473 --> 00:00:08.947 position:63%
less than just one bit per character, which is kind of mind-boggling if you think about

00:00:08.947 --> 00:00:09.150 position:63%
it.

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:12.983 position:63%
There's actually an open cash prize for text compression (it's known

00:00:12.983 --> 00:00:16.567 position:63%
as the Hutter prize) available to anyone who can beat the current best

00:00:16.567 --> 00:00:20.050 position:63%
record for compressing a gigabyte of Wikipedia as small as possible.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.052 position:63%
I promise we'll build up to what exactly this animation is showing in due time,

00:00:25.052 --> 00:00:29.880 position:63%
the core idea that it conveys This underlies well I was going to say it underlies

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:35.003 position:63%
several of the winning submissions here but that's not true it's actually the backbone

00:00:35.003 --> 00:00:39.890 position:63%
for all of them every single one and the method is not even specific to text it is

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:44.601 position:63%
a fully general technique that you could use to compress images or videos audio

00:00:44.601 --> 00:00:49.547 position:63%
really any data type and this algorithm also got me thinking differently about that

00:00:49.547 --> 00:00:54.434 position:63%
phrase compression is intelligence but I'll save the discussion for that until the

00:00:54.434 --> 00:00:59.263 position:63%
end As with any compression algorithm, you can think of this as two complementary

00:00:59.263 --> 00:01:04.032 position:63%
programs: one used by a sender to encode text into ones and zeros (preferably as

00:01:04.032 --> 00:01:08.978 position:63%
few as possible), and the other used by the receiver to decode that bit stream back

00:01:08.978 --> 00:01:09.626 position:63%
into text.

00:01:10.076 --> 00:01:13.637 position:63%
And the goal here is lossless compression, meaning that output needs to

00:01:13.637 --> 00:01:17.197 position:63%
be an exact replica of the original input, and winners for that Hutter

00:01:17.197 --> 00:01:21.611 position:63%
Prize are not just determined based on the size of this this compressed representation,

00:01:21.611 --> 00:01:24.670 position:63%
it also takes into account the size of the decoding program.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:28.920 position:63%
So imagine yourself right now setting out to win this prize.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:30.273 position:63%
Where would you start?

00:01:30.317 --> 00:01:32.570 position:63%
How would you even begin to concoct such an algorithm?

00:01:33.404 --> 00:01:37.896 position:63%
One option would be to think deeply about the fundamental limit of compressibility;

00:01:37.896 --> 00:01:42.604 position:63%
after all, understanding the nature of that limit might inform how to get close to it.

00:01:42.954 --> 00:01:45.704 position:63%
We've covered this in a previous chapter, but it's worth a quick recap.

00:01:46.053 --> 00:01:49.800 position:63%
Back in the nineteen forties, Claude Shannon showed that the number of

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:53.761 position:63%
bits used for an optimal compression of a message should be approximately

00:01:53.761 --> 00:01:57.454 position:63%
the negative log base two of the probability of seeing that message.

00:01:58.003 --> 00:02:02.173 position:63%
This negative log quantity is what he dubbed What he defined as the information

00:02:02.173 --> 00:02:06.448 position:63%
of an event, and I've said before, I kind of wish we had adopted a convention of

00:02:06.448 --> 00:02:09.034 position:63%
calling this the log base one half of p instead,

00:02:09.034 --> 00:02:13.099 position:63%
since I think that better captures the core property that this expression is

00:02:13.099 --> 00:02:16.846 position:63%
trying to convey, namely each time a probability gets chopped in half,

00:02:16.846 --> 00:02:19.274 position:63%
the information content increases by one bit.

00:02:20.334 --> 00:02:23.976 position:63%
So for example, a message with a probability of one half

00:02:23.976 --> 00:02:27.684 position:63%
raised to the power k has exactly k bits of information.

00:02:28.667 --> 00:02:32.667 position:63%
Looking at Shannon's result, on the one hand this is very intuitive:

00:02:32.667 --> 00:02:37.667 position:63%
more likely messages require fewer bits, and less likely messages require more bits.

00:02:37.968 --> 00:02:41.538 position:63%
And the more precise statement itself is also decently intuitive:

00:02:41.538 --> 00:02:46.317 position:63%
each time one more bit is required, it corresponds to a message that's half as likely.

00:02:46.968 --> 00:02:50.043 position:63%
On the other hand though, if you really think seriously about

00:02:50.043 --> 00:02:53.017 position:63%
what this whole result is saying, it seems utterly absurd.

00:02:53.724 --> 00:02:56.024 position:63%
Take a moment to think about some passage with

00:02:56.024 --> 00:02:58.474 position:63%
a large number of highly predictable characters.

00:02:59.074 --> 00:03:02.274 position:63%
Say I give you the opening to the preamble of the US Constitution.

00:03:02.874 --> 00:03:06.863 position:63%
After these first few words, you-an intelligent viewer familiar with famous

00:03:06.863 --> 00:03:10.959 position:63%
historical documents-can predict with a very high confidence what's going to

00:03:10.959 --> 00:03:15.374 position:63%
follow pretty much as soon as the context of what's being quoted has become clear.

00:03:15.846 --> 00:03:18.417 position:63%
Say you give a fifty percent chance to all of

00:03:18.417 --> 00:03:21.046 position:63%
these characters following that first phrase.

00:03:21.596 --> 00:03:24.488 position:63%
That would mean that the Shannon information of

00:03:24.488 --> 00:03:27.196 position:63%
those remaining characters is just one bit.

00:03:27.696 --> 00:03:31.099 position:63%
Even if you gave a one in a thousand chance to those being the specific

00:03:31.099 --> 00:03:34.646 position:63%
characters that arise, that's still only ten bits of Shannon information.

00:03:35.246 --> 00:03:40.338 position:63%
But now try to imagine writing a program that converts text into ones and zeros,

00:03:40.338 --> 00:03:44.602 position:63%
the prospect of somehow cramming most of a Really, full paragraph,

00:03:44.602 --> 00:03:48.866 position:63%
down into just one bit, or even ten bits, feels, well, ridiculous.

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:53.113 position:63%
For comparison, the default encoding using ASCII would

00:03:53.113 --> 00:03:56.130 position:63%
spend over twenty five hundred bits on these words.

00:03:56.780 --> 00:03:59.220 position:63%
Some of you may have heard of Huffman encodings,

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:03.694 position:63%
a more efficient way to associate individual characters with bit strings such that more

00:04:03.694 --> 00:04:05.880 position:63%
common characters get shorter bit strings.

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:09.758 position:63%
Using the specific Huffman encoding I have illustrated on the left,

00:04:09.758 --> 00:04:13.439 position:63%
that only gets you down to around fifteen hundred bits for this section.

00:04:13.439 --> 00:04:18.359 position:63%
Even if you try to get smarter by applying a Huffman encoding To larger chunks of letters

00:04:18.359 --> 00:04:22.725 position:63%
instead-something like the tokens used by modern language models-it just seems

00:04:22.725 --> 00:04:26.705 position:63%
completely intractable to hope that most of a full paragraph like this,

00:04:26.705 --> 00:04:30.354 position:63%
no matter how predictable, could ever fit into just a single bit,

00:04:30.354 --> 00:04:32.454 position:63%
or even into a small handful of bits.

00:04:33.104 --> 00:04:36.954 position:63%
And yet, by the end of this video, you're going to see exactly this happening.

00:04:38.222 --> 00:04:40.884 position:63%
The issue with any method, like a Huffman encoding,

00:04:40.884 --> 00:04:44.067 position:63%
that always turns a given character into a fixed bit string,

00:04:44.067 --> 00:04:46.572 position:63%
is that it does nothing to incorporate context.

00:04:47.121 --> 00:04:52.600 position:63%
Claude Shannon's result is telling us compressibility is entirely tied to predictability,

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:57.772 position:63%
but predictability of a given word can vary a lot depending on what came before it.

00:04:58.508 --> 00:05:01.541 position:63%
Now, sitting here in the 2020s, if I told you, hey,

00:05:01.541 --> 00:05:06.239 position:63%
we need to leverage the predictability of text based on the context around it,

00:05:06.239 --> 00:05:10.758 position:63%
there's a very obvious tool at our disposal, namely a large language model.

00:05:11.276 --> 00:05:14.548 position:63%
And actually, the large part here doesn't matter, and if anything,

00:05:14.548 --> 00:05:17.226 position:63%
as we'll see later, small ones have a real advantage.

00:05:17.626 --> 00:05:22.156 position:63%
A language model is specifically trained to take in an arbitrary piece of text and

00:05:22.156 --> 00:05:26.575 position:63%
make a prediction of what comes next, where that prediction takes the form of a

00:05:26.575 --> 00:05:31.326 position:63%
probability distribution over a set of all possible choices for what might come next.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:35.334 position:63%
But even if I just hand you some black box prediction machine,

00:05:35.334 --> 00:05:39.150 position:63%
it's really not obvious how you would turn that into a compression algorithm.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.983 position:63%
Looking back at our setup with a sender and a receiver,

00:05:42.983 --> 00:05:47.083 position:63%
the critical addition we're going to make for this whole video is to assume that

00:05:47.083 --> 00:05:51.131 position:63%
each one has a copy of the same language model that can be used as part of that

00:05:51.131 --> 00:05:52.700 position:63%
encoding and decoding process.

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:57.358 position:63%
The best way to think about this algorithm is that it's something where you can

00:05:57.358 --> 00:06:01.231 position:63%
kind of slot in an arbitrary language model, and then the predictions that model

00:06:01.231 --> 00:06:05.200 position:63%
makes are going to be used many times for the encoding and the decoding processes,

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:08.930 position:63%
and basically the better the model you use, the better the final compression.

00:06:10.092 --> 00:06:15.239 position:63%
The 10,000 high-foot view of what this algorithm is actually going to do is that it

00:06:15.239 --> 00:06:20.758 position:63%
encodes an arbitrary piece of text into one highly highly specific real number between 0

00:06:20.758 --> 00:06:21.192 position:63%
and 1.

00:06:21.342 --> 00:06:25.092 position:63%
I recognize that might sound absurd at first, but it's actually very beautiful.

00:06:25.292 --> 00:06:26.592 position:63%
Let me show you how it works.

00:06:31.796 --> 00:06:35.630 position:63%
Even though most modern language models process text as a sequence of tokens,

00:06:35.630 --> 00:06:38.120 position:63%
which are common chunks of characters like words,

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:42.054 position:63%
illustrating this algorithm is going to be much much easier if we use one that

00:06:42.054 --> 00:06:43.996 position:63%
processes text character by character.

00:06:44.710 --> 00:06:47.240 position:63%
That is, you feed in an arbitrary string of text,

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:51.010 position:63%
and what it spits out is a probability distribution over all characters.

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:55.235 position:63%
In fact, for maximal simplicity and complete reduced visual clutter,

00:06:55.235 --> 00:06:58.099 position:63%
I'm going to limit the output to just be 26 letters,

00:06:58.099 --> 00:07:00.909 position:63%
ignoring capitalization and neglecting punctuation.

00:07:01.484 --> 00:07:04.907 position:63%
To be clear, everything about the actual algorithm we'll describe works

00:07:04.907 --> 00:07:08.234 position:63%
just as well if you're using a model with a larger token vocabulary.

00:07:08.584 --> 00:07:11.850 position:63%
In fact, we have to switch to one later to see how effective it is;

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:14.384 position:63%
this just makes our diagrams much easier to follow.

00:07:16.404 --> 00:07:18.628 position:63%
We'll start off with a completely empty input,

00:07:18.628 --> 00:07:21.480 position:63%
meaning the distribution it spits out on the other side is

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:25.203 position:63%
the model's prediction for what character starts an arbitrary piece of text.

00:07:25.654 --> 00:07:29.680 position:63%
And instead of a bar chart like this, throughout the video we'll visualize

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:33.434 position:63%
probability distributions as horizontal stacks of side-by-side bars,

00:07:33.434 --> 00:07:38.004 position:63%
where the width of a given bar represents the probability of seeing a given letter.

00:07:38.453 --> 00:07:41.463 position:63%
The total width of this diagram is then one, since adding up the

00:07:41.463 --> 00:07:44.474 position:63%
probabilities of every possible possible outcome should be one.

00:07:44.774 --> 00:07:48.573 position:63%
In fact, the essence of the whole algorithm is going to be to imagine this

00:07:48.573 --> 00:07:52.373 position:63%
stack as living on the unit interval of the number line from zero to one.

00:07:52.823 --> 00:07:56.023 position:63%
And you think of each of these possible first characters

00:07:56.023 --> 00:07:58.823 position:63%
as occupying a range of values on that interval.

00:07:59.474 --> 00:08:03.917 position:63%
Notice a more common starting letter like T occupies a larger range of numbers,

00:08:03.917 --> 00:08:06.730 position:63%
whereas this bar for P is about a fourth as wide,

00:08:06.730 --> 00:08:09.823 position:63%
meaning the model sees that as four times less likely.

00:08:09.823 --> 00:08:11.044 position:63%
That's likely to be the starting letter.

00:08:11.444 --> 00:08:15.294 position:63%
And then its neighbor q gets an absolutely puny region by comparison.

00:08:15.794 --> 00:08:20.224 position:63%
What we're going to set up here is a way to associate not just individual characters,

00:08:20.224 --> 00:08:24.394 position:63%
but arbitrarily long strings of text with very narrow regions on this interval.

00:08:25.244 --> 00:08:29.186 position:63%
Then encoding the text will look like representing a very specific real

00:08:29.186 --> 00:08:33.294 position:63%
number inside the appropriate tiny region and sending it to the receiver.

00:08:34.578 --> 00:08:39.078 position:63%
Here, let's step through what it looks like to encode a specific small word like "math.

00:08:40.404 --> 00:08:43.898 position:63%
If instead of a blank input you feed in a single letter M_ to the model,

00:08:43.898 --> 00:08:47.004 position:63%
here's what the resulting output looks like for its prediction.

00:08:47.604 --> 00:08:49.754 position:63%
And you'll notice it's dominated by the vowels.

00:08:50.104 --> 00:08:54.430 position:63%
A_ gets a thirty eight percent chance, E_ gets a sixteen percent chance, and so on.

00:08:54.430 --> 00:08:57.884 position:63%
Which makes sense, it would be unlikely to see a consonant following

00:08:57.884 --> 00:09:00.220 position:63%
the letter M_ And the way I'm drawing things,

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:03.674 position:63%
where the second probability stack is perfectly lined up under that

00:09:03.674 --> 00:09:06.975 position:63%
M_ gives the width of each bar in the second layer a really nice

00:09:06.975 --> 00:09:08.397 position:63%
Really nice interpretation.

00:09:08.397 --> 00:09:13.411 position:63%
Going from the full width of the diagram down to the width of this m involves scaling by

00:09:13.411 --> 00:09:18.482 position:63%
the probability of starting with m, and then to shrink further down to the width of this

00:09:18.482 --> 00:09:23.439 position:63%
A requires scaling by the probability of seeing an A given that you started with an m.

00:09:23.499 --> 00:09:26.669 position:63%
That product right there is exactly the probability of

00:09:26.669 --> 00:09:30.074 position:63%
starting with the two letters m A according to the model.

00:09:30.374 --> 00:09:32.223 position:63%
This is the chain rule from probability.

00:09:32.806 --> 00:09:36.101 position:63%
Likewise, these other small widths represent the probability

00:09:36.101 --> 00:09:39.506 position:63%
that the model gives to starting with m e_ or m i_ and so on.

00:09:40.205 --> 00:09:41.906 position:63%
And there's no reason to stop at two layers.

00:09:42.356 --> 00:09:44.856 position:63%
If you feed into the model the context m a_ here's

00:09:44.856 --> 00:09:47.256 position:63%
the distribution of what it thinks will follow.

00:09:47.656 --> 00:09:51.332 position:63%
And then again the width of any bar in this third layer is telling you the

00:09:51.332 --> 00:09:55.356 position:63%
probability that the model gives to starting with a given three letter sequence.

00:09:56.422 --> 00:10:01.071 position:63%
Applying the same idea one more time to the prefix m a t_ We get a distribution

00:10:01.071 --> 00:10:05.955 position:63%
over all possible fourth letters and the width of this little bar here corresponds

00:10:05.955 --> 00:10:10.545 position:63%
to the probability that the model gives to the whole text starting with math.

00:10:11.097 --> 00:10:13.544 position:63%
And this is what I mean when I say that a narrow region

00:10:13.544 --> 00:10:15.946 position:63%
on the interval corresponds to a given piece of text.

00:10:16.446 --> 00:10:19.604 position:63%
If I give you some number in this range, really any number,

00:10:19.604 --> 00:10:23.939 position:63%
together with the fact that I intend to encode a word with only four characters,

00:10:23.939 --> 00:10:27.097 position:63%
you can unambiguously recover what word I'm talking about.

00:10:27.597 --> 00:10:29.991 position:63%
Zooming out one layer, you can see that other ranges

00:10:29.991 --> 00:10:32.247 position:63%
correspond to other strings starting with m a t.

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:37.026 position:63%
Zooming out even more from that, you can see ranges of values

00:10:37.026 --> 00:10:40.680 position:63%
corresponding to strings starting just with the letters M_A_ and so on.

00:10:42.218 --> 00:10:45.398 position:63%
Once we're zoomed out this far, it's hard to actually see the tiny

00:10:45.398 --> 00:10:48.868 position:63%
ranges past a couple of levels given how small those probabilities are.

00:10:49.368 --> 00:10:51.748 position:63%
But from up here, looking at the full unit interval,

00:10:51.748 --> 00:10:55.319 position:63%
we can see what it looks like to range over all possible four letter strings.

00:10:55.819 --> 00:11:00.033 position:63%
And of course, displaying four layers is completely arbitrary; in principle,

00:11:00.033 --> 00:11:04.468 position:63%
every infinite string corresponds to a unique real number between zero and one.

00:11:08.344 --> 00:11:12.781 position:63%
And so back to our sender and receiver, the idea of the compression algorithm is that I,

00:11:12.781 --> 00:11:16.764 position:63%
the sender, am going to package up a real number representing my text together

00:11:16.764 --> 00:11:20.444 position:63%
with the number of characters in that text and I pass them along to you.

00:11:20.895 --> 00:11:23.961 position:63%
In a moment we'll dig into representing that real number in binary

00:11:23.961 --> 00:11:26.795 position:63%
and how to analyze the number of bits of precision required.

00:11:27.444 --> 00:11:31.175 position:63%
But first it might be worth being clear on what exactly it looks like for you as

00:11:31.175 --> 00:11:35.045 position:63%
the receiver to use this number together with the shared language model to decode.

00:11:35.045 --> 00:11:35.833 position:63%
To decode the text.

00:11:36.532 --> 00:11:39.573 position:63%
The decompression algorithm is also something where you imagine

00:11:39.573 --> 00:11:42.855 position:63%
slotting in this text predictor, and it's critically important that

00:11:42.855 --> 00:11:46.233 position:63%
it be exactly the same text predictor that was used for the encoding.

00:11:47.233 --> 00:11:50.367 position:63%
Step one is to run the language model on a blank context to get

00:11:50.367 --> 00:11:53.500 position:63%
this initial distribution of characters, and from that you can

00:11:53.500 --> 00:11:56.733 position:63%
deduce that the number I gave you lands inside the region for m.

00:11:57.848 --> 00:12:01.240 position:63%
You now know to run the model a second time using M_ as the context,

00:12:01.240 --> 00:12:05.230 position:63%
that gives you this new distribution, and by looking at where my special number

00:12:05.230 --> 00:12:09.170 position:63%
falls in the second layer, you can deduce that the second letter of my message

00:12:09.170 --> 00:12:12.461 position:63%
is an A_ That in turn tells you what to plug into the model next,

00:12:12.461 --> 00:12:16.451 position:63%
and you just repeat this a number of times equal to however many letters I told

00:12:16.451 --> 00:12:17.449 position:63%
you are in my word.

00:12:19.665 --> 00:12:23.866 position:63%
If you want, you can avoid the need to send that message length explicitly if you

00:12:23.866 --> 00:12:27.964 position:63%
introduce a special symbol into the vocabulary that represents end of message.

00:12:28.464 --> 00:12:32.714 position:63%
In that case the decoding procedure would look like zooming in until you hit that symbol.

00:12:33.165 --> 00:12:36.084 position:63%
This is a design decision, in many ways that's more elegant,

00:12:36.084 --> 00:12:39.927 position:63%
but for our purposes I'm going to separate out that message length explicitly,

00:12:39.927 --> 00:12:43.964 position:63%
mainly because the introductory examples become cleaner if they're just pure text.

00:12:44.728 --> 00:12:47.221 position:63%
When Shannon was describing optimal compression,

00:12:47.221 --> 00:12:51.013 position:63%
one of the key ideas was that a perfectly compressed message should look

00:12:51.013 --> 00:12:54.753 position:63%
indistinguishable from random noise, and you can get a feel for how the

00:12:54.753 --> 00:12:58.597 position:63%
algorithm we're building up here fits this property by choosing uniformly

00:12:58.597 --> 00:13:00.779 position:63%
random real numbers between zero and one.

00:13:01.546 --> 00:13:05.446 position:63%
What you'll notice is that the strings these correspond to don't look arbitrary.

00:13:05.946 --> 00:13:09.401 position:63%
Even though every possible string is represented in this diagram,

00:13:09.401 --> 00:13:12.696 position:63%
almost all of the ones that we're landing on look reasonable;

00:13:12.696 --> 00:13:15.247 position:63%
they look like actual snippets of English text.

00:13:15.597 --> 00:13:19.845 position:63%
And that's because the widths of the bars in this diagram are defined such that more

00:13:19.845 --> 00:13:24.144 position:63%
likely strings, according to the model, take up a larger portion of the number line,

00:13:24.144 --> 00:13:28.342 position:63%
meaning if you just pick a spot at random, it's very likely to land on a plausible

00:13:28.342 --> 00:13:28.747 position:63%
string.

00:13:28.747 --> 00:13:29.016 position:63%
and on a likely string.

00:13:29.716 --> 00:13:32.321 position:63%
In fact, this is basically just a way to visualize

00:13:32.321 --> 00:13:34.666 position:63%
how it is that language models produce text.

00:13:35.117 --> 00:13:38.145 position:63%
And so far I've been writing these real numbers down as decimals,

00:13:38.145 --> 00:13:41.965 position:63%
but to think of this as an actual encoding, we're going to have to represent them

00:13:41.965 --> 00:13:45.784 position:63%
in binary as ones and zeros, potentially a lot of ones and zeros depending on the

00:13:45.784 --> 00:13:46.716 position:63%
length of the text.

00:13:47.819 --> 00:13:50.348 position:63%
Just to be clear on what I mean by binary here,

00:13:50.348 --> 00:13:54.330 position:63%
in the same way that the first digit after the decimal point in our usual

00:13:54.330 --> 00:13:57.935 position:63%
base ten system tells you which tenth of the interval you fall in,

00:13:57.935 --> 00:14:02.132 position:63%
the first bit after a point in binary representation tells you whether you're

00:14:02.132 --> 00:14:04.069 position:63%
in the left half or the right half.

00:14:04.368 --> 00:14:08.477 position:63%
The next bit after that tells you whether you're in the left half or the right half of

00:14:08.477 --> 00:14:12.490 position:63%
that section, and then the third bit narrows you down to one specific eighth of the

00:14:12.490 --> 00:14:12.968 position:63%
interval.

00:14:12.977 --> 00:14:13.776 position:63%
So, and so on.

00:14:14.176 --> 00:14:17.127 position:63%
Each new bit chops the space of possibilities in half.

00:14:17.676 --> 00:14:21.077 position:63%
So the highlighted section here represents the range of real

00:14:21.077 --> 00:14:24.308 position:63%
numbers that you could specify starting with these bits,

00:14:24.308 --> 00:14:28.276 position:63%
or in other words, every possible continuation of this binary string.

00:14:28.877 --> 00:14:32.949 position:63%
Whenever we choose to stop adding more bits, giving just a finite representation,

00:14:32.949 --> 00:14:37.374 position:63%
the number that you get is the one sitting on the leftmost endpoint of this highlighted

00:14:37.374 --> 00:14:37.776 position:63%
region.

00:14:38.360 --> 00:14:43.321 position:63%
Now if we bring back the pieces of text under this diagram that we were just looking at,

00:14:43.321 --> 00:14:48.001 position:63%
the binary number that I've chosen to write down here is one that sits inside that

00:14:48.001 --> 00:14:52.511 position:63%
narrow region for the string MATH, and it uses a total of 10 bits of precision.

00:14:53.110 --> 00:14:55.471 position:63%
By zooming out, you can actually see that 9 bits

00:14:55.471 --> 00:14:58.029 position:63%
could not be enough to encode this particular word.

00:14:58.029 --> 00:15:02.161 position:63%
The closest 9-bit number on the left is over here, well outside the target region.

00:15:02.598 --> 00:15:05.569 position:63%
And then the next nine bit number after that overshoots,

00:15:05.569 --> 00:15:09.495 position:63%
including that extra tenth bit narrows down the highlighted region enough

00:15:09.495 --> 00:15:13.898 position:63%
so that we can get right in the middle of these two, landing in that target range.

00:15:14.348 --> 00:15:17.963 position:63%
And in fact, from this point of view, considering the size of that target

00:15:17.963 --> 00:15:20.737 position:63%
region compared to the spacing between ten bit numbers,

00:15:20.737 --> 00:15:24.798 position:63%
it kind of feels like we lucked out being able to use only ten bits in this case.

00:15:25.630 --> 00:15:30.282 position:63%
Ten bits for a four-letter word works out to be an average of 2.5 bits per character,

00:15:30.282 --> 00:15:33.948 position:63%
which is not terrible, but the real advantage kicks in as we apply

00:15:33.948 --> 00:15:36.630 position:63%
this method to longer and longer pieces of text.

00:15:37.130 --> 00:15:39.170 position:63%
Let me show you what it looks like for this process

00:15:39.170 --> 00:15:40.930 position:63%
to play out over the full word mathematics.

00:15:41.680 --> 00:15:46.730 position:63%
You'll notice that each new letter it sees, the e, the m, the a, are highly predictable.

00:15:47.080 --> 00:15:50.480 position:63%
And as such, the size of our target region hardly shrinks at all.

00:15:50.614 --> 00:15:52.937 position:63%
It's only by the time you get to that final S

00:15:52.937 --> 00:15:55.414 position:63%
that it feels like an actual decision was made.

00:15:55.814 --> 00:16:00.159 position:63%
And in fact, that same 10-bit string that we use to represent the word math still

00:16:00.159 --> 00:16:04.664 position:63%
falls in this region for mathematics, bringing our bits per character down to 0.91.

00:16:05.718 --> 00:16:09.626 position:63%
Actually, that's a little disingenuous, because in addition to sending the real number,

00:16:09.626 --> 00:16:11.468 position:63%
we also have to send the message length.

00:16:11.818 --> 00:16:15.318 position:63%
That is, after all, what distinguishes whether we're sending math versus mathematics.

00:16:15.618 --> 00:16:18.518 position:63%
But still, this is starting to look like actually good compression.

00:16:19.018 --> 00:16:22.018 position:63%
And I was actually really surprised when I saw this particular example.

00:16:22.368 --> 00:16:25.114 position:63%
I knew that we should get something good with mathematics,

00:16:25.114 --> 00:16:27.766 position:63%
but if using only ten bits was a little lucky for math,

00:16:27.766 --> 00:16:30.418 position:63%
it is extremely lucky for this longer word mathematics.

00:16:30.870 --> 00:16:34.148 position:63%
The small region for this word has a width of 2^-18,

00:16:34.148 --> 00:16:39.066 position:63%
whereas the points on the number line that are representable with ten bits or

00:16:39.066 --> 00:16:41.020 position:63%
fewer are spaced out by 2^-10.

00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:45.276 position:63%
In essence, what you're looking at is the fact that our word should have required

00:16:45.276 --> 00:16:48.921 position:63%
eighteen bits of information, and yet we lucked out being able to represent it with ten

00:16:48.921 --> 00:16:49.170 position:63%
bits.

00:16:49.720 --> 00:16:52.689 position:63%
In fact, it's worth taking a moment to think about how many bits,

00:16:52.689 --> 00:16:56.070 position:63%
in general, it should require to represent a point inside a given region.

00:17:01.424 --> 00:17:05.672 position:63%
Notice how all of the tick marks for values expressible with two bits

00:17:05.672 --> 00:17:10.104 position:63%
or fewer are spaced out by one fourth, and those expressible with three

00:17:10.104 --> 00:17:14.229 position:63%
bits or fewer are spaced out by one eighth, and in general all the

00:17:14.229 --> 00:17:18.723 position:63%
numbers expressible with k bits or fewer are spaced out by exactly 2^-k.

00:17:19.124 --> 00:17:23.932 position:63%
So if you have some target region of size p and you don't want to rely on luck,

00:17:23.932 --> 00:17:28.558 position:63%
you want a guarantee that you'll be able to land inside that target region,

00:17:28.558 --> 00:17:33.306 position:63%
Using a number expressible in k bits (or fewer) regardless of how this region

00:17:33.306 --> 00:17:37.688 position:63%
is positioned, what you need is to be sure that 2^-k is smaller than p.

00:17:38.638 --> 00:17:43.468 position:63%
Rearranging a bit, this is the same as saying negative k is at most log base 2 of p,

00:17:43.468 --> 00:17:48.528 position:63%
which in turn is the same as saying k is greater than or equal to the negative log base

00:17:48.528 --> 00:17:48.988 position:63%
2 of p.

00:17:49.462 --> 00:17:52.329 position:63%
So in other words, if your text has a probability p,

00:17:52.329 --> 00:17:55.747 position:63%
and you round up the negative log base 2 of that probability,

00:17:55.747 --> 00:17:59.827 position:63%
you are guaranteed to be able to represent your text with this many bits,

00:17:59.827 --> 00:18:01.812 position:63%
and possibly fewer if you're lucky.

00:18:02.910 --> 00:18:05.160 position:63%
Now this expression should feel very familiar.

00:18:05.610 --> 00:18:09.774 position:63%
It looks just like Shannon's definition for the information of a given message,

00:18:09.774 --> 00:18:14.413 position:63%
and that's really the whole beauty here: Shannon proved in principle why no compression

00:18:14.413 --> 00:18:17.839 position:63%
algorithm can encode text with fewer bits than this, on average,

00:18:17.839 --> 00:18:21.002 position:63%
and he even showed why it should be possible, in principle,

00:18:21.002 --> 00:18:23.110 position:63%
to get arbitrarily close to this limit.

00:18:23.824 --> 00:18:27.179 position:63%
But what we're looking at here feels, at least at first glance,

00:18:27.179 --> 00:18:29.895 position:63%
like it realizes Shannon's dream: it's a concrete,

00:18:29.895 --> 00:18:34.261 position:63%
implementable method where the size of a compressed message is basically the same

00:18:34.261 --> 00:18:36.924 position:63%
as the information value, up to a rounding error.

00:18:37.590 --> 00:18:41.290 position:63%
To be more exact, this is only describing the encoded real number.

00:18:41.740 --> 00:18:43.940 position:63%
Remember, we also need to send that message length.

00:18:44.340 --> 00:18:46.790 position:63%
But that message length will be very small by comparison.

00:18:47.240 --> 00:18:51.391 position:63%
For example, if you allocate a fixed header of 32 bits to describe that length,

00:18:51.391 --> 00:18:55.490 position:63%
this would leave you safe to encode messages up to 4 billion characters long.

00:18:55.890 --> 00:18:58.886 position:63%
So at large scales, essentially all of the encoding size

00:18:58.886 --> 00:19:01.990 position:63%
is tied up in the bits of precision for that real number.

00:19:02.490 --> 00:19:04.590 position:63%
And it's the number of those bits that rubs right out.

00:19:04.590 --> 00:19:05.660 position:63%
It was right up against Shannon's limit.

00:19:06.768 --> 00:19:08.926 position:63%
And in fact you've already seen how there are times when

00:19:08.926 --> 00:19:10.968 position:63%
it seems to do a little better than Shannon's limit.

00:19:11.318 --> 00:19:14.191 position:63%
Our example of mathematics had 18 bits of information,

00:19:14.191 --> 00:19:17.118 position:63%
but it just happened to overlap with the 10 bit value.

00:19:17.818 --> 00:19:21.623 position:63%
In a much more extreme case, a message that consists entirely of the

00:19:21.623 --> 00:19:25.205 position:63%
letter A would correspond to the leftmost point of the diagram,

00:19:25.205 --> 00:19:29.346 position:63%
which in our scheme is actually what the empty bitstring would represent.

00:19:29.346 --> 00:19:32.088 position:63%
So in that extreme example all of the actual content

00:19:32.088 --> 00:19:34.778 position:63%
is tied up purely in really in the message length.

00:19:34.778 --> 00:19:37.874 position:63%
The reason these examples don't violate Shannon's theorem is

00:19:37.874 --> 00:19:40.971 position:63%
because his theorem is all about averages; it's saying that

00:19:40.971 --> 00:19:44.480 position:63%
you can't do better than this negative log p expression on average.

00:19:44.480 --> 00:19:48.638 position:63%
All these cases of getting lucky are balanced out by the fact that more typically

00:19:48.638 --> 00:19:52.488 position:63%
you have to round up that fractional information to a larger whole number.

00:19:53.838 --> 00:19:56.476 position:63%
However, there's a much deeper reason that this encoding

00:19:56.476 --> 00:19:58.738 position:63%
size does not actually achieve Shannon's limit.

00:19:59.696 --> 00:20:02.600 position:63%
This quantity, the negative log p that we wrote down,

00:20:02.600 --> 00:20:05.669 position:63%
Is not actually the Shannon information of the message,

00:20:05.669 --> 00:20:10.545 position:63%
because this value p is representing the probability of the text according to the model,

00:20:10.545 --> 00:20:13.066 position:63%
not the true probability of seeing that text,

00:20:13.066 --> 00:20:15.915 position:63%
say as sampled by what a typical sender might send.

00:20:16.315 --> 00:20:19.725 position:63%
In this sense the final compression rate depends almost entirely

00:20:19.725 --> 00:20:22.335 position:63%
on the quality of the language model plugged in,

00:20:22.335 --> 00:20:26.065 position:63%
and how close its predictions are to the true statistics of language.

00:20:26.713 --> 00:20:30.245 position:63%
And this is actually a nice moment to step back and reflect on how one

00:20:30.245 --> 00:20:33.675 position:63%
benefit of learning about this whole algorithm is that it offers an

00:20:33.675 --> 00:20:37.913 position:63%
interesting perspective on how language models are even trained in the first place.

00:20:38.749 --> 00:20:41.178 position:63%
So if you look at this key conclusion we have,

00:20:41.178 --> 00:20:45.298 position:63%
that the compression size looks like the negative log of the probability that

00:20:45.298 --> 00:20:49.945 position:63%
the model gives to a given piece of text, you can expand that probability as a product,

00:20:49.945 --> 00:20:53.906 position:63%
namely a product over all the individual probabilities for each successive

00:20:53.906 --> 00:20:55.649 position:63%
prediction that the model makes.

00:20:55.799 --> 00:20:58.082 position:63%
And whenever you see a product in a log like this,

00:20:58.082 --> 00:21:02.055 position:63%
it's always nice to break it up as a sum, in this case the sum of the negative logs of

00:21:02.055 --> 00:21:03.699 position:63%
all those individual probabilities.

00:21:03.699 --> 00:21:04.667 position:63%
those individual predictions.

00:21:05.567 --> 00:21:08.052 position:63%
Now you can think of each one of these terms as something

00:21:08.052 --> 00:21:10.667 position:63%
like a cost associated with one of the model's predictions.

00:21:11.317 --> 00:21:14.909 position:63%
When the model gives a low probability to the true next character,

00:21:14.909 --> 00:21:18.447 position:63%
the cost is very high, whereas if it gives a high probability to

00:21:18.447 --> 00:21:21.767 position:63%
that true next character, the cost is low, approaching zero.

00:21:22.265 --> 00:21:25.124 position:63%
I imagine this sounds very familiar to many viewers,

00:21:25.124 --> 00:21:29.852 position:63%
because in the last chapter we talked all about how models are specifically optimized

00:21:29.852 --> 00:21:34.581 position:63%
to minimize this value, or rather the average of this value over all tokens that they

00:21:34.581 --> 00:21:36.615 position:63%
see during the pre-training process.

00:21:36.965 --> 00:21:39.594 position:63%
In fact, the average of this value over all tokens

00:21:39.594 --> 00:21:42.065 position:63%
sampled from language even has a special name.

00:21:42.165 --> 00:21:43.515 position:63%
It's called the cross-entropy.

00:21:43.715 --> 00:21:48.226 position:63%
More specifically, it's the cross-entropy of the model's distribution of language,

00:21:48.226 --> 00:21:49.931 position:63%
against the true distribution.

00:21:50.681 --> 00:21:53.881 position:63%
Now the point I want to make is that there are two ways you can think about pre-training.

00:21:54.231 --> 00:21:58.469 position:63%
The typical framing says that the goal is to create a next-token predictor,

00:21:58.469 --> 00:22:01.181 position:63%
and this cross-entropy acts as a loss function.

00:22:01.381 --> 00:22:05.149 position:63%
It is the way to turn that goal into a numerical score that can be minimized.

00:22:05.149 --> 00:22:08.713 position:63%
But through the lens of this algorithm that we're talking about today,

00:22:08.713 --> 00:22:12.124 position:63%
a mathematically equivalent, but I think psychologically distinct,

00:22:12.124 --> 00:22:15.331 position:63%
framing is to say that the objective is not prediction per se.

00:22:15.577 --> 00:22:18.277 position:63%
It's to create the best possible text compressor.

00:22:18.477 --> 00:22:22.977 position:63%
In that first framing, that use of cross-entropy initially feels like a design decision.

00:22:23.327 --> 00:22:26.477 position:63%
You know, you have to choose some loss function and it's one choice out of many.

00:22:26.677 --> 00:22:30.813 position:63%
But in the second framing, it's much more obvious what number needs to be minimized.

00:22:30.865 --> 00:22:34.477 position:63%
It's the number of bits that each training example gets compressed into.

00:22:34.677 --> 00:22:35.627 position:63%
Simple as that.

00:22:35.777 --> 00:22:38.320 position:63%
I don't know about you, but I just found this a much nicer way to

00:22:38.320 --> 00:22:40.981 position:63%
think about what's really going on during pre- During pre training.

00:22:41.881 --> 00:22:45.727 position:63%
What this means for the story of the compression algorithm is that it's only as good

00:22:45.727 --> 00:22:49.481 position:63%
as the prediction model you plug into it, and we can actually have some fun here.

00:22:49.831 --> 00:22:53.462 position:63%
Let me show you two different encodings of the same example phrase,

00:22:53.462 --> 00:22:57.038 position:63%
namely the concept of proof and its associated mathematical rigor

00:22:57.038 --> 00:23:00.831 position:63%
began in ancient Greek mathematics, exemplified in Euclid's Elements.

00:23:01.433 --> 00:23:05.742 position:63%
This first encoding comes from NanoGPT, a small 3.7 million parameter toy model

00:23:05.742 --> 00:23:09.833 position:63%
that we've been using throughout this video for the sake of illustrations.

00:23:10.283 --> 00:23:14.683 position:63%
It uses 254 bits in total, which averages to around 2 bits per character,

00:23:14.683 --> 00:23:18.660 position:63%
which is double what Claude Shannon estimated should be possible,

00:23:18.660 --> 00:23:22.879 position:63%
and I want to draw your attention specifically to the last two words.

00:23:22.936 --> 00:23:26.319 position:63%
The thing is, if you have a background in math and its history,

00:23:26.319 --> 00:23:28.735 position:63%
Those last two words are highly predictable.

00:23:29.035 --> 00:23:32.255 position:63%
Now by contrast, this is what the encoding looks like using

00:23:32.255 --> 00:23:35.585 position:63%
"quen," an open source model with half a billion parameters.

00:23:36.335 --> 00:23:39.582 position:63%
Notice how those last two words now only cost 5 bits,

00:23:39.582 --> 00:23:43.135 position:63%
and the overall average is less than 1 bit per character.

00:23:44.083 --> 00:23:47.801 position:63%
As an even more extreme example, let me bring back that preamble to the U.S.

00:23:47.801 --> 00:23:48.383 position:63%
Constitution.

00:23:49.033 --> 00:23:52.941 position:63%
After that first phrase, which itself uses about 35 bits,

00:23:52.941 --> 00:23:58.356 position:63%
everything that follows is so predictable that it hardly adds anything at all,

00:23:58.356 --> 00:23:59.933 position:63%
only 10 bits in total.

00:24:00.583 --> 00:24:04.407 position:63%
Contrast that with the Huffman encoding I showed at the very beginning of the video,

00:24:04.407 --> 00:24:06.683 position:63%
and that is a two order of magnitude improvement.

00:24:07.681 --> 00:24:11.179 position:63%
This one is especially fun to see in the context of our diagram:

00:24:11.179 --> 00:24:15.879 position:63%
Gwen processes tokens instead of characters, meaning there are many many more options

00:24:15.879 --> 00:24:20.416 position:63%
in each probability distribution, and zeroing in on that first token 'we' seems to

00:24:20.416 --> 00:24:24.897 position:63%
cost about 11 bits, and likewise getting to that second token 'the' takes another

00:24:24.897 --> 00:24:25.881 position:63%
dozen or so bits.

00:24:26.781 --> 00:24:29.725 position:63%
'People' is a little more predictable, and there's a bit

00:24:29.725 --> 00:24:32.931 position:63%
more zooming in still as we fill out 'of the United States'.

00:24:33.529 --> 00:24:37.431 position:63%
But after that initial phrase, notice how each new token dominates

00:24:37.431 --> 00:24:41.273 position:63%
almost all of the probability space, meaning we only very rarely

00:24:41.273 --> 00:24:44.879 position:63%
have to update the actual real number encoding our position.

00:24:45.329 --> 00:24:47.934 position:63%
If you look carefully, it seems like most of the decisions

00:24:47.934 --> 00:24:50.179 position:63%
come down to capitalization and spelling choices.

00:24:50.729 --> 00:24:55.129 position:63%
For example, the choice to spell defense with a C rather than an S costs us a couple bits.

00:24:55.779 --> 00:25:00.079 position:63%
But at a high level, high predictability really has turned into high compressibility.

00:25:02.434 --> 00:25:06.556 position:63%
Hang on a second," I hear some of you saying, "isn't it kind of disingenuous

00:25:06.556 --> 00:25:10.515 position:63%
to praise an algorithm for compressing this passage so much if it's just

00:25:10.515 --> 00:25:14.529 position:63%
calling out to some massive model that's basically memorized that passage

00:25:14.529 --> 00:25:18.434 position:63%
somewhere in its hundreds of millions of weights?" And yes, absolutely.

00:25:18.784 --> 00:25:20.084 position:63%
In fact, hold on to that thought.

00:25:20.184 --> 00:25:21.334 position:63%
We'll come back to it in just a minute.

00:25:21.836 --> 00:25:26.176 position:63%
A second practical concern that I imagine is niggling in the minds of some viewers

00:25:26.176 --> 00:25:29.722 position:63%
is how if you really keep up this process and you keep zooming in,

00:25:29.722 --> 00:25:33.850 position:63%
it is very quickly going to become problematic if you need some ultra-precise

00:25:33.850 --> 00:25:38.190 position:63%
representation of real numbers, not just the one real number that we're encoding,

00:25:38.190 --> 00:25:42.795 position:63%
but also all of the other values delineating the bounds of each successive probability

00:25:42.795 --> 00:25:43.536 position:63%
distribution.

00:25:44.684 --> 00:25:47.789 position:63%
And indeed, a naive literal interpretation of what these

00:25:47.789 --> 00:25:50.784 position:63%
diagrams show would not yet be a practical algorithm.

00:25:51.334 --> 00:25:56.396 position:63%
The idea, as I've shown it so far, dates back at least to 1960 with some unpublished

00:25:56.396 --> 00:26:00.133 position:63%
work by Peter Elias, but it wasn't made practical until 1976,

00:26:00.133 --> 00:26:05.134 position:63%
when Jorma Rissanen and Richard Pascoe independently developed a workable version,

00:26:05.134 --> 00:26:08.811 position:63%
one that avoids a need for ever-growing numerical precision,

00:26:08.811 --> 00:26:11.884 position:63%
giving what's known today as an arithmetic coding.

00:26:12.484 --> 00:26:16.161 position:63%
The core idea involves renormalizing the interval you're looking at at each step

00:26:16.161 --> 00:26:19.653 position:63%
to always have a length of one, pretty much analogous to the way that we've

00:26:19.653 --> 00:26:23.284 position:63%
been animating it throughout the video to always fill the width of the screen.

00:26:23.584 --> 00:26:27.034 position:63%
I'll leave links in the description for anyone curious about implementation details.

00:26:27.734 --> 00:26:30.491 position:63%
The upshot is that arithmetic codes offer a nearly

00:26:30.491 --> 00:26:33.304 position:63%
perfect way to turn a predictor into a compressor.

00:26:33.360 --> 00:26:37.684 position:63%
This is the structure used for all of those Hutter Prize winners that I mentioned earlier.

00:26:37.684 --> 00:26:39.616 position:63%
that I referenced, where improvements generally

00:26:39.616 --> 00:26:41.754 position:63%
came from plugging in better and better predictors.

00:26:42.822 --> 00:26:47.071 position:63%
I would be remiss not to mention that there is another completely different approach

00:26:47.071 --> 00:26:51.422 position:63%
for turning a predictor into a compressor like this, published in 2013 by Yarik Duda.

00:26:51.722 --> 00:26:55.329 position:63%
It's known as an asymmetric numeral system, it's also very beautiful,

00:26:55.329 --> 00:26:59.145 position:63%
and it avoids any of these precision issues with real numbers by instead

00:26:59.145 --> 00:27:01.967 position:63%
encoding a message with a large ever growing integer,

00:27:01.967 --> 00:27:06.672 position:63%
applying a few other tricks as well, resulting in a much faster overall encoding process.

00:27:07.436 --> 00:27:10.436 position:63%
And if we ended things right here, I think that's a fine lesson.

00:27:10.936 --> 00:27:13.136 position:63%
Arithmetic codes are very beautiful on their own.

00:27:13.586 --> 00:27:16.743 position:63%
They make a core idea from information theory a lot more concrete,

00:27:16.743 --> 00:27:20.762 position:63%
and they offer an alternate perspective on how to even think about language models,

00:27:20.762 --> 00:27:22.436 position:63%
both at inference and in training.

00:27:22.886 --> 00:27:27.336 position:63%
But this is part three in a trilogy that I gave the title, Compression is Intelligence.

00:27:33.548 --> 00:27:37.298 position:63%
Now this phrase is something that's hard to analyze rigorously-intelligence is

00:27:37.298 --> 00:27:41.048 position:63%
very ill defined, lots of people will disagree about what precisely it means.

00:27:41.498 --> 00:27:43.937 position:63%
I think the better way to engage with it is to

00:27:43.937 --> 00:27:46.748 position:63%
treat it like a proposed definition of intelligence.

00:27:47.298 --> 00:27:51.425 position:63%
Suppose you wanted to give a mathematical foundation to this otherwise very

00:27:51.425 --> 00:27:56.048 position:63%
squishy and human concept, is compression alone really enough to carry that burden?

00:27:56.934 --> 00:28:01.411 position:63%
By analogy, I think of it like when Newton gave the word "energy" a mathematical meaning,

00:28:01.411 --> 00:28:05.184 position:63%
this measurable quantity with units of kilogram meters per second squared.

00:28:05.634 --> 00:28:08.019 position:63%
When he made this definition, it was reductive:

00:28:08.019 --> 00:28:12.534 position:63%
it misses out on a lot of what people mean when they use the word "energy" colloquially.

00:28:12.984 --> 00:28:16.517 position:63%
And yet, despite this more limited scope, it was still productive for

00:28:16.517 --> 00:28:20.151 position:63%
him to make that definition, and, reductive though it might have been,

00:28:20.151 --> 00:28:23.684 position:63%
it still managed to unify a number of seemingly disparate phenomena.

00:28:23.684 --> 00:28:27.011 position:63%
Disparate concepts: Similarly any attempt to give a word like

00:28:27.011 --> 00:28:30.829 position:63%
"intelligence" a mathematical meaning seems bound to miss out on some

00:28:30.829 --> 00:28:34.920 position:63%
of the uniquely human nuance that our everyday usage bakes into that word.

00:28:35.320 --> 00:28:39.127 position:63%
But the question is whether any such definition, even if reductive,

00:28:39.127 --> 00:28:44.070 position:63%
is still productive, and manages to unify seemingly disparate aspects of intelligence.

00:28:45.222 --> 00:28:48.597 position:63%
What I want to close with here are three specific ways that compression

00:28:48.597 --> 00:28:51.972 position:63%
captures part of what I think I mean when I use the word intelligence.

00:28:52.522 --> 00:28:56.030 position:63%
Where it plays into the topic for today is that in all three cases,

00:28:56.030 --> 00:29:00.637 position:63%
having an algorithm like an arithmetic coding freshly in our minds makes the connection

00:29:00.637 --> 00:29:01.422 position:63%
more concrete.

00:29:02.388 --> 00:29:06.646 position:63%
To number one, compression and prediction are really two sides of the same coin,

00:29:06.646 --> 00:29:10.638 position:63%
and prediction, in turn, seems a lot more clearly related to intelligence.

00:29:11.038 --> 00:29:14.553 position:63%
Arithmetic codes show one half of this equivalence; specifically,

00:29:14.553 --> 00:29:18.988 position:63%
they offer a nearly perfect way to turn an arbitrary predictor into a compressor.

00:29:19.764 --> 00:29:21.314 position:63%
And you can go the other way around too.

00:29:21.714 --> 00:29:24.788 position:63%
Suppose you have an arbitrary text compressor and you want to use

00:29:24.788 --> 00:29:27.814 position:63%
it to make predictions about what might follow a given passage.

00:29:28.014 --> 00:29:31.183 position:63%
I won't go into the full details, but here's the basic idea:

00:29:31.183 --> 00:29:34.775 position:63%
for any potential string that might follow, you ask how much larger

00:29:34.775 --> 00:29:38.314 position:63%
the compressed message would be with that addition versus without.

00:29:39.334 --> 00:29:42.815 position:63%
If you let δ represent the difference between these two,

00:29:42.815 --> 00:29:46.793 position:63%
then 2^-δ gives you a pretty good approximation for the implied

00:29:46.793 --> 00:29:50.834 position:63%
predictive model's guess about how probable that next string is.

00:29:51.484 --> 00:29:54.853 position:63%
Doing this over all strings in a given token vocabulary, for example,

00:29:54.853 --> 00:29:59.004 position:63%
could give you a probability distribution analogous to the type we get out of modern

00:29:59.004 --> 00:29:59.834 position:63%
language models.

00:30:00.540 --> 00:30:04.704 position:63%
So, in the sense that predictors can be turned into compressors and vice versa,

00:30:04.704 --> 00:30:07.603 position:63%
compression and prediction really are equivalent, and,

00:30:07.603 --> 00:30:11.714 position:63%
insofar as good prediction seems to require having a coherent model of what's

00:30:11.714 --> 00:30:15.825 position:63%
really going on, this hints at the idea that compression ratios may be a good

00:30:15.825 --> 00:30:17.090 position:63%
proxy for intelligence.

00:30:18.043 --> 00:30:21.759 position:63%
This also ties to an influential theory in modern neuroscience known as

00:30:21.759 --> 00:30:25.685 position:63%
predictive processing: it posits that the brain is continuously generating

00:30:25.685 --> 00:30:29.453 position:63%
top-down predictions for sensory signals and then what gets passed back

00:30:29.453 --> 00:30:33.536 position:63%
up from the sensory organs is not the full information of what they received,

00:30:33.536 --> 00:30:37.043 position:63%
it's just the error between the prediction and the sensed reality.

00:30:37.543 --> 00:30:41.550 position:63%
The upshot of this theory is that much of what we experience as intelligence

00:30:41.550 --> 00:30:45.136 position:63%
may just boil down to neurological predictions and And compression.

00:30:46.136 --> 00:30:49.386 position:63%
But there are aspects of intelligence, or at the very least what I

00:30:49.386 --> 00:30:52.735 position:63%
think I mean when I use that word, the prediction alone would miss.

00:30:53.436 --> 00:30:58.272 position:63%
Personally, I feel like I understand something better when it becomes smaller in my mind;

00:30:58.272 --> 00:31:01.587 position:63%
when a handful of facts, or even just one, are all I need to

00:31:01.587 --> 00:31:04.086 position:63%
remember or rederive a wide swath of results.

00:31:04.795 --> 00:31:08.349 position:63%
Progress in fields like physics also feels like this, having fewer,

00:31:08.349 --> 00:31:12.646 position:63%
shorter laws feels like a step forward, even if they make identical predictions.

00:31:13.774 --> 00:31:16.973 position:63%
This becomes more measurable in a machine learning context:

00:31:16.973 --> 00:31:20.280 position:63%
if you have two models with equivalent predictive abilities,

00:31:20.280 --> 00:31:23.804 position:63%
but one requires trillions of parameters and the other is small,

00:31:23.804 --> 00:31:27.924 position:63%
it suggests that the smaller one is relying on fewer memorized assumptions.

00:31:28.624 --> 00:31:31.684 position:63%
Now, if you plug each one of these into an arithmetic coding,

00:31:31.684 --> 00:31:34.343 position:63%
if we assume each has equivalent prediction ability,

00:31:34.343 --> 00:31:38.758 position:63%
that would mean that they compress a given large data set, say a gigabyte of Wikipedia,

00:31:38.758 --> 00:31:39.912 position:63%
Down to the same size.

00:31:40.212 --> 00:31:42.675 position:63%
After all, the whole point of this video so far is that

00:31:42.675 --> 00:31:45.361 position:63%
that compression ratio is dependent only on the prediction.

00:31:46.588 --> 00:31:51.286 position:63%
However, an honest account of the final compression ratio here should really take into

00:31:51.286 --> 00:31:56.038 position:63%
account the size of the decoder program, which in this case includes the model itself.

00:31:56.638 --> 00:32:00.758 position:63%
After all, if you actually wanted to send somebody a gigabyte of Wikipedia in

00:32:00.758 --> 00:32:04.985 position:63%
a compressed package, the raw data stream coming out of that arithmetic coding

00:32:04.985 --> 00:32:08.838 position:63%
would be useless if you didn't also give them the means of decoding it.

00:32:09.396 --> 00:32:13.417 position:63%
This is, as I referenced earlier, how the Hutter Prize scores its results.

00:32:13.417 --> 00:32:16.317 position:63%
If you didn't include the decoding program size in this,

00:32:16.317 --> 00:32:20.461 position:63%
you could basically cheat by having such a decoding program just include a hard

00:32:20.461 --> 00:32:24.656 position:63%
copy of all of Wikipedia in its source code, and then the supposed payload could

00:32:24.656 --> 00:32:25.795 position:63%
be arbitrarily small.

00:32:26.495 --> 00:32:30.330 position:63%
If earlier it felt like cheating to have a huge model kind of memorize the U.S.

00:32:30.330 --> 00:32:32.491 position:63%
Constitution and use that for prediction, this

00:32:32.491 --> 00:32:34.745 position:63%
right here is how that would get accounted for.

00:32:35.374 --> 00:32:38.126 position:63%
When you measure things this way, it means a small model,

00:32:38.126 --> 00:32:42.374 position:63%
with equivalent predictive capacity to a larger one, would count as better compression.

00:32:43.124 --> 00:32:47.597 position:63%
And this at the very least rhymes with the idea that a clearer understanding uses a

00:32:47.597 --> 00:32:52.124 position:63%
smaller number of general principles, instead of a large number of memorized facts.

00:32:52.524 --> 00:32:55.849 position:63%
And that to me is what makes the phrase "compression is intelligence" a

00:32:55.849 --> 00:32:59.174 position:63%
lot more thought provoking than "prediction is intelligence" would be.

00:33:00.148 --> 00:33:03.149 position:63%
A third aspect of what I think I mean when I use the word

00:33:03.149 --> 00:33:06.098 position:63%
intelligence is an ability to learn new things quickly.

00:33:06.598 --> 00:33:10.348 position:63%
The smartest people I know are able to dive into a new field with relative ease.

00:33:11.086 --> 00:33:13.261 position:63%
And again, at first glance this would seem not

00:33:13.261 --> 00:33:15.436 position:63%
at all to be captured by a compression ratio.

00:33:16.086 --> 00:33:20.314 position:63%
If we stay focused on our example of a predictive model plugged into an arithmetic

00:33:20.314 --> 00:33:23.717 position:63%
coding, even if you measure things the way the Hutter Prize does,

00:33:23.717 --> 00:33:26.347 position:63%
where you include the size of the decoder program,

00:33:26.347 --> 00:33:30.420 position:63%
nothing about that final compression size depends on how long it took to train

00:33:30.420 --> 00:33:32.586 position:63%
that underlying model in the first place.

00:33:33.336 --> 00:33:37.586 position:63%
Except there actually is a case where learning rate influences this final score.

00:33:38.331 --> 00:33:42.548 position:63%
Suppose you start with a set-up where the underlying predictive model was trained on

00:33:42.548 --> 00:33:46.665 position:63%
some specific data set, like Wikipedia, but then later you want to apply the same

00:33:46.665 --> 00:33:50.882 position:63%
encoder and decoder to a different kind of text, maybe encoding all of Shakespeare.

00:33:51.632 --> 00:33:54.322 position:63%
Since the underlying model was trained in a different context,

00:33:54.322 --> 00:33:57.489 position:63%
you would expect it to perform worse in this different setting-certainly

00:33:57.489 --> 00:34:00.482 position:63%
worse than a model that was tailor trained to Shakespearean English.

00:34:01.076 --> 00:34:03.974 position:63%
But here's a clever way that we can tweak the algorithm:

00:34:03.974 --> 00:34:08.010 position:63%
rather than having the model stay static during the full compression process,

00:34:08.010 --> 00:34:09.976 position:63%
what if you fine-tuned it on the fly?

00:34:10.326 --> 00:34:13.826 position:63%
You let it adapt to the particular tokens that it sees along the way.

00:34:14.375 --> 00:34:16.972 position:63%
Concretely, this would mean with each new token it sees,

00:34:16.972 --> 00:34:20.775 position:63%
you would apply backpropagation to tweak all of the weights based on what it saw.

00:34:21.226 --> 00:34:24.697 position:63%
What this would mean is that over the course of the compression process,

00:34:24.697 --> 00:34:27.976 position:63%
the model would get better and better tailored to that new context.

00:34:27.976 --> 00:34:28.145 position:63%
Text.

00:34:28.545 --> 00:34:31.321 position:63%
In order for this to work, over on the decoding side you

00:34:31.321 --> 00:34:34.196 position:63%
would have to have the decoder do exactly the same thing.

00:34:34.496 --> 00:34:37.113 position:63%
As long as all of the updates are deterministic,

00:34:37.113 --> 00:34:40.548 position:63%
the way that the model would get updated on the receiving side

00:34:40.548 --> 00:34:44.746 position:63%
would be an identical replay of the way that it was updated during encoding.

00:34:45.346 --> 00:34:48.746 position:63%
This is important because the predictions at each step have to stay identical.

00:34:49.096 --> 00:34:51.896 position:63%
This is actually an idea used in practice, and in fact

00:34:51.896 --> 00:34:54.696 position:63%
you can avoid the need to send any parameters at all.

00:34:54.708 --> 00:34:59.140 position:63%
If you have the encoder and decoder start off by generating a completely random model,

00:34:59.140 --> 00:35:03.159 position:63%
but pseudo random in a way that's identical for both of them, in either case,

00:35:03.159 --> 00:35:07.024 position:63%
when you do this the final compression rate depends not just on the static

00:35:07.024 --> 00:35:11.507 position:63%
predictive capacity, but also on how quickly the model is adapting to the new context,

00:35:11.507 --> 00:35:14.908 position:63%
that is the better the learning rate, the better the compression.

00:35:16.916 --> 00:35:19.637 position:63%
So, zooming out, if we look over the full trilogy,

00:35:19.637 --> 00:35:24.426 position:63%
the conclusion from Part 1 was that when Shannon began studying compression rigorously,

00:35:24.426 --> 00:35:26.766 position:63%
he was forced to engage with intelligence.

00:35:27.166 --> 00:35:30.343 position:63%
Pure data analysis was not enough; he could only estimate the

00:35:30.343 --> 00:35:33.416 position:63%
compressibility of English by surveying human predictions.

00:35:34.016 --> 00:35:38.138 position:63%
The conclusion of Part 2 was that the modern engineering for artificial intelligence

00:35:38.138 --> 00:35:41.916 position:63%
uses mathematical tools that were originally developed to study compression.

00:35:42.356 --> 00:35:46.691 position:63%
Together, these two alone hint at a connection between compression and intelligence;

00:35:46.691 --> 00:35:50.356 position:63%
it would appear that studying one forces you to engage with the other.

00:35:51.106 --> 00:35:55.855 position:63%
For this third part, the upshot is that one of the best compression algorithms we have,

00:35:55.855 --> 00:36:00.167 position:63%
say as measured by the Hutter prize, requires using a model which is effective

00:36:00.167 --> 00:36:03.606 position:63%
at prediction while being small and which also learns quickly.

00:36:04.506 --> 00:36:07.756 position:63%
So are compression and intelligence just really the same thing?

00:36:08.412 --> 00:36:11.012 position:63%
The safe answer is of course "no," just as Newton's

00:36:11.012 --> 00:36:13.612 position:63%
definition of energy is not the same as Webster's.

00:36:13.912 --> 00:36:16.974 position:63%
But if you did need a mathematical definition for intelligence,

00:36:16.974 --> 00:36:20.133 position:63%
my own takeaway here is that I was just genuinely surprised that

00:36:20.133 --> 00:36:23.438 position:63%
compression takes off many more boxes than I would have guessed the

00:36:23.438 --> 00:36:27.812 position:63%
first time that I ever thought about the seemingly innocent task of making files smaller.

00:36:32.532 --> 00:36:35.521 position:63%
If you enjoy these videos and you want to support the team behind them,

